  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "MLive surveys the growing backlash to Flock Safety's license plate reader network across Michigan, documenting vandalism, contract rejections (Lansing, Bay City, Eastpointe), misuse incidents, a Genesee County transparency lawsuit, and proposed state legislation (HB 5493) that would restrict ALPR use and impose 14-day retention. The piece also details how data-sharing works, an audit finding that Grand Rapids officers used Kalamazoo cameras for immigration enforcement, and unresolved Fourth Amendment questions.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "In Kalamazoo, police learned through an audit that Grand Rapids officers used Kalamazoo's cameras for immigration enforcement, which violated the department's use policy.",
      "paragraph_idx": 22
    },
    {
      "quote": "I keep saying that there's no guardrails. This is the Wild West.",
      "paragraph_idx": 25
    },
    {
      "quote": "Lansing City Council in August approved a resolution asking its police department to sever all contracts with Flock and shut down the city's 26 cameras.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "government agencies would be required to delete data after 14 days, publish privacy policies online and produce quarterly usage reports",
      "paragraph_idx": 40
    }
  ],
